WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.690
John "The Tooz" Matuszak was just as famous
in the '80s for his hard-partying, larger-than-life

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.920
persona as he was for his football game.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.740
But even if you didn't know him for either
of those reasons, you definitely knew about

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.330
his alter-ego the enormous, gentle Sloth,
whose unconventional appearance was the face

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.540
of the 1985 classic The Goonies.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.680
In a lot of ways, Sloth wasn't just the on-screen
alter ego of the former football star, he

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.720
was the actual manifestation of the person
Matuszak was on the inside: a lovable giant

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.380
who was also a generous friend.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.340
After attending a few different colleges,
including the University of Missouri - where

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.620
he got kicked out of for punching a guy at
a party - he landed at the University of Tampa.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.740
The NFL took notice of his 6 foot 8 frame
- so much so that the Houston Oilers selected

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.420
number one overall in the 1973 draft.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.680
Unfortunately, though, the man behind Sloth
didn't get his happy ending.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.200
During his second season with the Oilers,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.180
Matuszak was traded to the Kansas City Chiefs,
who traded him to the Washington Redskins,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.780
who cut him just two weeks later, at the end
of the 1976 preseason.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.880
Why was his stint in Washington so short-lived?

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.820
According to coach George Allen:
"Vodka and Valium."

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.320
"One for me...breakfast of champions, there."

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.700
This reputation for drink and debauchery would
only get worse.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:22.060
"I didn't know anything about John Matuszak
other than he was supposed to be nuts."

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.480
After Washington cut him loose, Matuszak was

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.499
a free agent, and the Oakland Raiders were
desperate after three of their defensive linemen

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.120
suffered season-ending injuries.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.920
So they pretended they didn't know about the
vodka and Valium and made Matuszak a Raider.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.189
Coach John Madden told ESPN:
"When he arrived, I wasn't about to talk to

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:41.140
him about his past.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.860
[...] If you know too much about a guy, you
tend to prejudge him, to hold his past against

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:45.360
him."

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.540
The gamble paid off.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.860
In Matuszak's first season, the Raiders won
their first Super Bowl title.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.620
But afterward, he gradually began to fall
back into his hard-partying ways.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.020
He always played well, though, so he managed
to get away with all the headline-making hijinks.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:03.280
Matuszak's ex-fiance Stephanie Cozart recounted
to the LA Times the number of times he committed

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.440
himself to rehab and then walked out before
completing the program.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:09.520
She revealed:
"I have a little velvet box at home.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.560
It's full of 30-day and 60-day chips.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.360
They give those out at the [AA and CA] meetings.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.820
His big goal was to get a 90-day chip [...] and
he never got one."

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.180
In his autobiography, Cruisin' with the Tooz,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.680
Matuszak described six separate incidents
involving automobiles that each resulted in

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.320
convictions.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.900
According to the LA Times, he had an excuse
for all of them, ranging from swerving to

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.900
avoid a child to not knowing there was marijuana
in the car.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.360
In one incident, he attacked the other driver
before fleeing the scene.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.380
But he never wanted to take responsibility
for his actions.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.460
According to ex-fiance Cozart:
"It was never his fault.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.900
And it never had anything to do with the fact
that he was high."

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.020
Football often leads to injuries and because

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.780
of one he sustained, Matuszak sat out the
1982 season and then decided to retire from

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.140
football altogether.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.400
The pain and resulting medications were the
final straw after years of hard partying and

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:01.960
drug abuse.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.519
In 1989 at the age of 38, he passed away after
accidentally overdosing on the painkiller

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.160
Darvocet.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.700
After the coroner's report was released, his
parents admitted that their son used prescription

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.640
and illegal drugs to try to cope with chronic
back pain.

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:19.120
They added in a statement:
"At the proper time, we will have things to

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:23.569
say about substance abuse and the scoundrels
in the medical profession who recklessly dispense

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:28.370
prescription drugs and the parasites of society
who distribute illicit drugs from back alleys

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.860
to fashionable condominiums."

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.140
In the midst of all of this, it's worth remembering

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.680
that John Matuszak had a big heart.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.980
He was a brawler, a partier, and a guy who
didn't want to take responsibility for his

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:41.360
actions, but that wasn't all.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.020
"Well I'm really not mean in personality at
all.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.600
When you play the game of pro football, it's
like assuming a character in a part."

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.230
He was also known for his generosity and often
visited sick children.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.880
Jeff Cohen, who played Chunk in Goonies shared:
"John was really nice to me and it was fun

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:58.480
to work with him.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.060
But it's funny, when I was a teenager and
I would start to watch the old NFL films [...] he

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.300
would just terrify people on the field, which
was totally shocking to me.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.100
I knew him as Sloth, the nice, lovable giant."

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.830
According to the New York Times, the coroner's
report also listed bronchial pneumonia and

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.060
an enlarged heart as factors that contributed
to his death.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:21.460
Unfortunately, The Tooz's big heart just couldn't
handle all the excesses it had been forced

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.280
to endure over the far too-short life of the
man it belonged to.

